<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Rousseau (journalist)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1716">1716</a>–<a href="/years/1785">1785</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> was a French writer and publisher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> who founded the Journal encyclopédique in Liège in 1756. The journal spread the ideas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>; forced out of Liège, Rousseau moved it in 1760 to Bouillon, where he also ran a publishing house.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The composer Johannes Ciconia, born in Liège in the fourteenth century, and the eighteenth-century opera composer André-Ernest-Modeste Grétry carried Liège music abroad, and in 1756 <b>Pierre Rousseau</b> founded the Journal encyclopédique in Liège, an important organ of the French Enlightenment, which later moved to Bouillon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
